<div class="unit-details">

    <!-- Overview & Objectives Split -->
    <div class="grid-2-col">
        <div class="content-box">
            <h2 class="content-title"><i class="fa-solid fa-binoculars"></i> Unit Overview</h2>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.5;">This unit introduces you to the idea that all
                digital information — numbers, text, images — is stored as patterns of 1s and 0s. You will learn
                why binary is used, how place value works in Base-2, and how to convert between binary and
                decimal. You will apply this knowledge through guided practice, short challenges, and simple
                data-representation activities such as encoding small messages or analysing how characters are
                stored. By the end, you will understand the link between number systems, logic, and how
                computers represent data.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-box">
            <h2 class="content-title"><i class="fa-solid fa-bullseye"></i> Unit Objectives</h2>
            <ul class="custom-list">
                <li>Explain why computers use binary to represent data.</li>
                <li>Convert between decimal (Base-10) and binary (Base-2) using place values.</li>
                <li>Understand the terms bit, byte, nibble, and file size units.</li>
                <li>Recognise how binary extends beyond numbers to represent characters (ASCII).</li>
                <li>Interpret and create small binary values (up to 8 bits).</li>
                <li>Apply binary skills in simple representation tasks, such as encoding or decoding short messages.</li>
                <li>Understand the role of binary as the foundation for all digital systems.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Concepts -->
    <div class="content-box">
        <h2 class="content-title"><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb"></i> Concepts</h2>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">This unit focuses on two SGS Computer Science concepts:</p>
        <div class="concepts-grid">
            <div class="concept-card">
                <h3 class="concept-name"><i class="fa-solid fa-database"></i> Data</h3>
                <p class="concept-def">Raw values that can be stored and processed.</p>
                <p class="concept-context"><strong>In this unit:</strong> Students see how numbers, letters, and
                    simple symbols are reduced to binary patterns inside a computer.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="concept-card">
                <h3 class="concept-name"><i class="fa-solid fa-diagram-project"></i> Logic</h3>
                <p class="concept-def">Rules that structure how data is represented and manipulated.</p>
                <p class="concept-context"><strong>In this unit:</strong> Students use place-value logic to
                    convert between number systems and reason about how binary structures information.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

